Subject: vm: read a shared memory's definition without its lock

Every host call that reaches into guest memory builds a MemoryView, and
building one asked the memory for its size and then for its definition.
For a shared memory, which is what a threaded guest runs on, each of
those took the read lock over the mapping: two lock acquisitions and two
lookups through the store per host call. In a profile of a guest under
load, those two accessors were 4.7% of the runtime's user-mode cycles,
the largest cost outside the guest and the syscalls themselves.

The lock protects the mapping. It does not protect what the two
accessors read. The definition is a fixed allocation made with the
mapping, either owned by the mapping or living in the instance, and
nothing replaces it while the mapping lives: grow, grow_at_least and
reset write the base and the length through it in place, and copy
builds a new mapping with a new one. So keep that pointer beside the
Arc, filled in where a shared memory is made and where one is copied,
and answer vmmemory, size and ty from it.

Reading the definition's contents without the lock is what already
happens. The compiled guest code holds no lock when it loads the base
from the definition, and for a static memory it does not load the bound
at all. Every caller of vmmemory here dereferences the pointer after the
guard has been dropped, MemoryView included: the pair of values it
bounds-checks against was already read outside the lock, and only the
size beside them was read inside it. A shared memory reserves all it can
grow to when it is made, which the patch before this one makes sure of,
so it never moves: a grow writes back the base that was already there
and only raises the length, which leaves a reader that races one with
the bound it would have had an instant earlier.

LinearMemory gains vmmemory_and_size, which answers with both at once
and defaults to the two accessors in turn, so that a caller that needs
both, which is every host call into guest memory, finds the memory in
the store once rather than twice. MemoryView::new uses it.

The same treatment goes to the file-descriptor backed memories in
sys-utils, which are the same code over a different mapping.

Unit tests cover the definition and the size agreeing with the mapping
before and after a grow, a clone reading what another handle grew, and a
copy having its own.

